{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And I will establish, etc. And all flesh shall not be cut off, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to repeat, saying <b>shall not be cut off</b> etc. and <b>shall not be</b> etc. And if it is to establish the matter by oath, behold we find that our Sages of blessed memory derived the matter only from this verse and the verse after it (Genesis 9:15), which says ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה [עוֹד] הַמַּיִם״ etc., and as I brought their words at the verse (Genesis 8:21) ״לֹא אוֹסִיף״. You are thereby shown that the duplication of ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה״ stated in this verse was necessary for its own sake." "<b>And it appears</b> to say according to their words in Sotah fol. 11, and this is their language: ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה״ etc. — Rabbi Ḥama bar Ḥanina said: Come, let us deal shrewdly with their deliverer, etc. Come, let us condemn them in water, for the Holy One, blessed be He, has already sworn that He will not bring a flood upon the world, as it says (Isaiah 54:9) ״כִּי מֵי נֹחַ זֹאת לִי״ etc. — but they do not know that upon the entire world as a whole He does not bring, but upon a single nation He does bring. Alternatively (אִי נַמִּי), He Himself does not bring, but they come and fall into it by themselves, and so it says ״וּמִצְרַיִם נָסִים״ etc. — end of quotation. Now, according to the first distinction, the versification of the verse is as follows: <b>And all flesh shall not be cut off any more by the waters of the flood</b> — meaning: HaShem will not bring a flood to destroy everything, but upon a portion, if they are liable, HaShem may bring upon them the evil of a flood. And He returned to say <b>and there shall not be</b> — meaning: and even if there should be a flood upon the portion, HaShem said that it shall not be to destroy the earth in the manner He did in the flood, which destroyed the very body of the earth. And it would not have sufficed to say ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה״ etc., for that is only a guarantee for the earth, not for man; therefore He said ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת.״ And accordingly, the two negations from which we learn the oath are: the first, ״לֹא יִכָּרֵת,״ and the second, ״לֹא יִהְיֶה,״ which is in the following verse — and there is no difficulty in the change of expression if the intent is one and the same, and concerning it the verse says ״לֹא יִהְיֶה הַדָּבָר.״" "And according to the approach of אִי נַמִּי, which distinguishes between whether He brings the flood or brings them into the waters as He did to Egypt — behold, there is room to doubt in the understanding of the Talmud whether it did not retract from the distinction it drew between all and a portion. And accordingly, even according to the reasoning of אִי נַמִּי, the resolution of the verse will be established so that it does not budge. Or perhaps the Talmud retracted from the first distinction and draws a different distinction, and there is no difference between all flesh and a portion of them. According to this approach, the verse will be explained in the manner they stated in Sanhedrin (101a), and this is their language: Rabbi Akiva said — if so, ״אַל תִּטַּמְּאוּ בְּכָל אֵלֶּה״ — here too, in all of them but not in one? Yes, rather in one of all these etc. — end of quotation. Accordingly, His saying <b>and all flesh shall not be cut off</b> means one of all flesh by the waters of the flood. And His saying <b>and there shall not be</b> etc. is to say: even without the destruction of living creatures, HaShem said that He would not destroy the earth, even a part of it, by the waters of the flood. Now, whether according to the first approach or the second approach, these two negations are necessary each for its own sake, and there is no redundancy of a negation twice; and therefore Rava needed to find two negations from two separate verses." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Rif, of blessed memory, who wrote in his halakhot, and this is his language: ״And that is what He said, 'a negation, a negation' — two times, as it is written ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה עוֹד מַבּוּל״ etc. ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה עוֹד הַמַּיִם לְמַבּוּל״״ — end of quotation. This implies that the Rif, of blessed memory, held that HaShem's statement ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה מַבּוּל לְשַׁחֵת הָאָרֶץ״ included both the earth and its inhabitants, and with this he counted two negations — namely, ״לֹא יִהְיֶה etc. לְשַׁחֵת הָאָרֶץ״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה [עוֹד] הַמַּיִם etc. לְשַׁחֵת כָּל בָּשָׂר,״ which are one matter. And according to his words, of blessed memory, let us return to consider: why did He repeat, saying ״לֹא יִכָּרֵת״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה״ in a single verse? Also, why not say that the two negations are ״לֹא יִכָּרֵת״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה״ of the first verse? And it appears to say that he holds the meaning of the verse is as follows: that HaShem swore that He would not cut off all flesh by any kind of evil in the world — not by fire and not by water and not the like. And His saying <b>by the waters of the flood</b> means: from the time of the waters of the flood. And He returned to say <b>and there shall never again be a flood</b> — even a single individual. And he followed the second approach that the Talmud stated — אִי נַמִּי, that He swore only not to bring it and not to bring them to it, and in any case the covenant concerning the flood applies even to a single individual. And this is difficult: if so, whence does one prove that in this matter there were two instances of a negation? And it is not so, for the negation ״לֹא יִהְיֶה [עוֹד] הַמַּיִם לְמַבּוּל״ explicitly states ״לְשַׁחֵת כָּל בָּשָׂר.״ And perhaps we can say that after HaShem revealed His intent not to bring a flood even upon a portion of the world, as is understood from the verse ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת etc. וְלֹא יִהְיֶה,״ we may return to explain His saying ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה מַבּוּל לְשַׁחֵת כָּל בָּשָׂר״ in the manner of His saying ״אַל תִּטַּמְּאוּ בְּכָל אֵלֶּה״ — that the intent is in one of these. But this approach is far-fetched: it leaves the superfluous phrase ״מִמֵּי הַמַּבּוּל״ without need, for the matter is self-understood that it is from the waters of the flood that He speaks." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Rosh, who wrote there, and this is his language: ״And it does not appear to me to be like the Rif's version etc., but these two verses that HaShem said to Noah etc. — ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל לִבּוֹ לֹא אוֹסִיף etc. וְלֹא אוֹסִיף״ etc. — you know that three negations are written: ״לֹא יִכָּרֵת״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה,״ and if so, we should need three, and if you combine them they would be five. Rather, it is certain that He counted only ״וַיֹֹאמֶר ה׳ אֶל לִבּוֹ״ etc. and similar to those that He said one after the other etc.״ — end of quotation. And his words, of blessed memory, are far from me to accept, for HaShem spoke to His heart, as it were, at that time when He smelled the pleasing fragrance, and afterward HaShem spoke what He had devised for good and made known to Noah His thought — and what proof is there from the verse ״לֹא אוֹסִיף״? Furthermore, the two negations were not stated in a single passage, as I wrote in its place. And as for his astonishment that they are three and in combination would be five — I beg his pardon, for the first two I already explained their meaning in their place, the reason for the doubling of the expression, and from those we do not derive even a single negation, because they are matters that He said in His mind to His heart, as it were. And if we attend to the principles of the law, even if there were two negations in a single passage, we would not derive from them — for a statement in the heart is not a statement, since a vow and an oath require verbal articulation, and one can prove only from HaShem's utterance that He spoke to Noah, which is the verse ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת״ etc. And therefore the Talmud proved only from the verse that HaShem spoke. And do we then combine the negations? Those that are stated are only two, as we settled above — for from the verse ״לֹא יִכָּרֵת״ there is no proof of two instances of a negation. Perforce, the words of the Rif are the foundation, whether according to our version and as we explained above." "<b>And behold,</b> from the sugya of Sotah the Talmud disclosed its view, for it was in doubt regarding the waters of the Flood — whether HaShem had cut a covenant with them that it would never come upon a portion of the created beings. And the Talmud conclusively determined that the Flood was excluded only from coming upon the created beings, but not from the coming of living creatures — rather, ״כִּי בָא סוּס וְגוֹ׳ בַיָּם״." "<b>And there remains</b> to stand upon the proof of clarifying the matter: whether the covenant is also upon other punishments — that they shall not come to cut off all flesh — or whether He swore specifically regarding the waters of the Flood." "<b>And in Midrash</b> Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 49:9) they said, and this is their language: ״חָלִילָה לְךָ״ etc. — You swore and said that You will not bring a Flood to the world; how do You outwit the oath? A Flood [of water] You do not bring, but one of fire You bring? If so, You have not fulfilled the obligation of the oath! ״הֲשׁוֹפֵט וְגוֹ׳ לֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״ etc. — If You seek a world — there is no justice; and if justice etc. — there is no world. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״אָהַבְתָּ צֶּדֶק״ etc. — until here. From this statement it is proven that He swore regarding all punishments, whether of water or of fire. And likewise it is proven from Zevachim (Zevachim 116a), that Balaam said to them that HaShem will bring neither of water nor of fire. And one needs to know what the force of Abraham's argument is — ״if so You have not fulfilled the obligation of the oath״ — if HaShem swore only regarding water and not regarding fire, when He brings of fire, His oath stands fulfilled." "Indeed, the intention is: what was the oath made for? So that the world would be secure, and the created beings would be fruitful and multiply, for they would not labor in vain — and without this they would give birth in panic. Moreover, what satisfaction of spirit was made for the world by the oath of the Flood if the destruction would come through something else? And if so, the obligation of the oath — that is, the matter that necessitated His swearing on its account — HaShem did not fulfill. And Abraham rebuked Avimelech concerning the covenant that exists both for a Flood and etc., even though he mentioned only the waters of the Flood. And the reason Abraham did not bring his proof from the verse ״לֹא אוֹסִיף״ etc. is that there no oath was stated, as we proved in our commentary, contrary to the words of the Rosh. And even according to the words of the Rosh, one may give a reason, for there too it is stated ״כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי״ and it enters into the ״כ״ of comparison together with the waters." "<b>Rather,</b> it is difficult according to the words of Abraham: was not the oath upon everything, and not upon a part of the world? And even according to what we explained — that the oath was also upon a part — that is specifically regarding the waters of the Flood, but regarding other punishments we find no hint of it. And according to what we explained as Abraham's reason — that He does not distinguish between water and fire — that is valid only for the destruction of everything, but regarding one individual matter, there is Elohim judging in the earth." "<b>Further</b> — I see that the Holy One, blessed be He, conceded to Abraham's argument yet did not stand by it, for after all He rained fire etc. ״וַיַּהֲפֹךְ ה׳״ etc. (Genesis 19:25). And if the oath was fulfilled even upon a portion, and even by fire, why did HaShem do in Sodom what He did?" "<b>Truly</b>, the truth of our Sages' words, may their memory be for blessing, is along this path: for Abraham advanced a argument of wisdom, namely, what reason did HaShem have to swear to bring a flood upon the world? In any case: if the creations are not liable, it is obvious that the El of justice would not act unjustly; but even if they do become liable to annihilation as the generation of the Flood became liable, even so HaShem would suppress the strict judgment and not execute a death sentence. This is the intent of the oath. From this you have learned that your intent is not to uphold the judgment to its full rigor — and if so, even in the matter before us HaShem will not uphold the judgment according to what would be warranted, and I will seek mercy, as he sought. And if your intent in the oath of the Flood was specifically regarding this particular element of water but not that of fire etc., and the maintenance of the line of justice exists without diminution, and if so here as well you would uphold the judgment and there is no place for my prayer — far be it from You etc. to deal craftily etc., as we explained above — for the creations who heard the oath reasoned that You would not uphold the judgments of justice to the end, and this would be cunning, Heaven forbid, and it would be unseemly for Him to deal craftily with His creatures, that they should understand one thing while He intends otherwise. And you will find that our Sages concluded their statement with the words of Abraham, and this is its language: ״If a world You seek — there is no justice, and if justice etc. — there is no world etc.; if You do not yield, צִבְחַר (the white, i.e., the merit of the righteous), the world cannot endure,״ end quote. Behold that Abraham's intent was to prove that there is no necessity that the judgment be executed in its completeness. And the Holy One, blessed be He, conceded the matter to him, and Abraham entered and asked that He act for the sake of the righteous and not destroy the wicked along with them — and this is צִבְחַר from the judgment — and HaShem was appeased on the matter. And when no side was found to tilt the judgment, it was sealed upon them to destroy them. And this particular element is not included in the oath of HaShem, and He rained upon the wicked fire and brimstone. And this the Egyptians saw, and they thought: ״כִּי בָאֵשׁ ה׳ ״ etc. (Isaiah 66:16) — to be cunning, so that they would not enter the judgment of fire, to be included in the world."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {4 "The Rosh's Aramaic-style phrases (״תֵּדַע דִּתְלָתָא לָאוֵי כְּתִיבֵי״ etc.) are translated as direct quotation, preserving their idiom as cited legal reasoning.", 7 "Citation בראשית רבה מט,ט normalized to Genesis Rabbah 49:9; the parenthetical זבחים קטז. refers to Zevachim 116a.", 8 "The phrase וְכוּ׳ (etc.) appears twice, leaving the elliptical referents to be inferred from context; rendered literally with ״etc.״ as in the original.", 11 "The Aramaic phrase אִי אַתְּ לָא תְוַתֵּר צִבְחַר לֵית עָלְמָא יָכִיל קַיָּים is rendered literally; צִבְחַר ('white') alludes to the merit/atonement that whites away sin. The final clause לִהְיוֹתָם פְּרָט בָּעוֹלָם is translated hyper-literally as 'to be included in the world.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:33.921872280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}